<h2>Immediate Safety Steps Inside Your Home</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Watch for electrical hazards.</strong> If water is near light fixtures, ceiling fans, outlets or the breaker panel, stay clear and turn off power to that area at the breaker only if you can reach the panel without standing in water. If in doubt, call an electrician.</li>
<li><strong>Catch the water.</strong> Place buckets, pots or storage bins under drips. A towel in the bottom of the bucket reduces splashing.</li>
<li><strong>Relieve a bulging ceiling carefully.</strong> If drywall is sagging with trapped water, the ceiling can collapse suddenly. Keep people and pets out of the room. Some homeowners puncture a small hole at the lowest point to let water drain into a bucket, but only do this if you can reach it safely and are not standing beneath a large bulge.</li>
<li><strong>Move belongings.</strong> Shift furniture, electronics, rugs and documents away from the wet area, and cover what cannot be moved with plastic sheeting.</li>
<li><strong>Check the attic only if it is safe.</strong> If you have a floored attic with good access and lighting, a bucket under the entry point can help. Step only on joists or decking, and stay out entirely during lightning.</li>
</ol>

