<h2>How We Handle Emergency Leaks</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Talk it through.</strong> When you call, we ask about the location of the leak, how heavily water is coming in, and the weather conditions, then advise you on interior protection.</li>
<li><strong>Temporary protection when it is safe.</strong> Once lightning and high winds have passed, a trained crew can install an emergency tarp or temporary patch over the affected area. Tarping is a stopgap, not a repair. It is meant to keep water out for a short time, and properly secured tarps avoid adding new nail holes in undamaged areas where possible.</li>
<li><strong>Free inspection and photo documentation.</strong> When the roof is dry, we inspect to find the true source, which is often some distance from where water appears inside, and photograph everything.</li>
<li><strong>Written estimate and permanent repair.</strong> You receive a written estimate describing the repair and options. Most leaks are corrected with a targeted <a href="/services/roof-repair-temple-tx/">roof repair</a>; widespread damage may call for <a href="/services/storm-damage-roof-repair-temple-tx/">storm damage repair</a> or replacement.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Why tarps should be installed by professionals</h3>
<p>Tarping a roof means working on a slick, sloped surface, often while it is still wet and windy, with a large sheet that catches wind like a sail. A poorly secured tarp can blow loose, tear shingles, or channel water to a new spot. Leave this job to a roofing crew with the right equipment and fall protection.</p>

<h2>Local Weather and Emergency Leaks in Temple</h2>
<p>Most emergency calls in Bell County follow severe thunderstorms during the spring storm season, roughly March through June, and during a secondary round in the fall. Heavy downpours can drop a lot of water in a short time, overwhelming gutters and exposing flashing weaknesses that never show up in light rain. Hail and strong straight-line winds can open up a roof in minutes. Hard freezes, such as the February 2021 winter storm, can also trigger leaks as ice melts and backs up at roof edges. Check the <a href="/weather/">Temple weather page</a> for current alerts before scheduling exterior work.</p>
<p>Leaks often show up first in older homes near downtown Temple, where flashing may be decades old, and in homes with complex rooflines where several slopes meet in valleys.</p>

<h2>After the Emergency: When to Get an Inspection</h2>
<p>Even if the dripping stops when the rain ends, the source is still there. Schedule an inspection promptly after any leak, because wet decking and insulation can lead to rot and mold if left alone. It is also wise to have the roof checked after any major storm, even when nothing has leaked yet. To arrange it, <a href="/free-roof-inspection/">request a free roof inspection</a> or call (512) 297-7580.</p>
